<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Pitt the Younger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1759">1759</a>–<a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Pitt the Younger was Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> from 1783 to 1801 and again from 1804 to 1806, taking office at the age of 24. Son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-elder">William Pitt the Elder</a>, he restored the public finances after the American war, introduced income tax and led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> against revolutionary and Napoleonic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He brought about the union with Ireland in 1801 but resigned when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> blocked Catholic emancipation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger.jpg" alt="William Pitt the Younger" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Hoppner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OlderPittThe_Younger.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William Pitt, the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-elder">William Pitt, 1st Earl of Chatham</a>, was born on 28 May 1759 at Hayes Place in Kent. His mother, Hester Grenville, was the sister of the former prime minister George Grenville. Often in poor health as a boy, he was educated at home by the Reverend Edward Wilson and became proficient in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek. He was admitted to Pembroke College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, on 26 April 1773, a month before turning fourteen, and there befriended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce">William Wilberforce</a>, who became a lifelong friend and ally in Parliament.</p><p>At the general election of September 1780 Pitt contested the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> seat and came bottom of the poll, having campaigned as an independent Whig. A by-election in the pocket borough of Appleby, controlled by James Lowther, sent him to the House of Commons in January 1781, ironically as he later railed against such boroughs. In the government of the Earl of Shelburne he was Chancellor of the Exchequer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The Fox–North Coalition fell in December 1783 after the King secured the defeat in the House of Lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a>'s bill to reform the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> entrusted the premiership to Pitt, after offering it three times before. In 1785 Pitt introduced a bill to remove thirty-six rotten and pocket boroughs and slightly widen the franchise, but it was defeated, and it was his last reform proposal. In May 1798 he fought a duel with George Tierney after accusing him of obstructing national defence.</p><p>He engineered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-union-1800">Acts of Union 1800</a> and tried to secure Catholic emancipation as part of the Union, but the King strongly opposed it as contrary to his coronation oath. Pitt resigned on 16 February 1801, so that Henry Addington could form a new administration.</p><p>During the opposition years of 1801 to 1804 he often stayed at Walmer Castle, organised a local Volunteer Corps against a French invasion and encouraged the construction of Martello towers and the Royal Military Canal. He returned to the premiership on 10 May 1804, but the King's opposition to including Charles James Fox weakened his second ministry. It renewed the alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> against Napoleonic France.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pitt, who had long suffered from gout and ill health, died on 23 January 1806 at Bowling Green House on Putney Heath, probably of peptic ulceration, unmarried and childless. Parliament agreed to pay his debts of £40,000, and he was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> on 22 February. He helped define the prime minister as the supervisor and coordinator of the government <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, and he rehabilitated the nation's finances after the American War of Independence. Wilberforce wrote that he had never known his equal for personal purity and love of his country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Pitt the Younger” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Whigs dominated government until the 1760s, when George III sought a more active role; long ministries followed under Lord North from 1770 and <b>William Pitt the Younger</b> from 1783.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The prime minister, <b>William Pitt the Younger</b>, and the chief secretary, Lord Castlereagh, concluded that only union with Great Britain could secure Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The rebellion of the Society of United Irishmen in 1798, in which many thousands died and a French force landed in County Mayo, convinced <b>William Pitt the Younger</b> that union was needed to secure Ireland during the war with revolutionary France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Hanoverian kings Sir Robert Walpole, in office from 1721 to 1742, became in effect the first prime minister, yet monarchs still shaped policy: George III’s opposition to Catholic emancipation forced <b>William Pitt the Younger</b> to resign in 1801.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Until the 1830s, however, the king still chose ministers and could sustain them, as George III sustained <b>William Pitt the Younger</b> against a hostile Commons majority in 1783–1784.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>William Pitt the Younger</b>'s income tax, set at two shillings in the pound on higher incomes, received royal assent to fund the war against Revolutionary France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-9">9 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kent, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.19,0.73&amp;year=1789" aria-label="Kent on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4567 390 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William Pitt the Younger"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5017 660h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5017 660h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.19,0.73&amp;year=1789">What was here in 1789? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I know that the interests of the two Countries must be taken together, and that a man cannot speak as a true Englishman, unless he speaks as a true Irishman, nor as a true Irishman, unless he speaks as a true Englishman…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech in the House of Commons on union with Ireland, 31 January 1799 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/65670/pg65670-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…we did not enter into this war for any purpose of ambition; our object was not to acquire, but to preserve; and in this sense, what we have gained by the war is, in one word, all that we should have lost without it…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech in the House of Commons on union with Ireland, 31 January 1799 &#183; original &#183; on the war with revolutionary France &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/65670/pg65670-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
